<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
        <RootNamespace>Vixen.Net</RootNamespace>
        <Description>
            Networking for Vixen: the transport contract every transport implements, the channel
            vocabulary the layers above speak, and the simulation decorator that makes a perfect
            in-process wire behave like a bad one. Optional — a single-player game that never
            references it pays nothing.
        </Description>
        <PackageTags>networking;multiplayer;transport;replication;game-engine;vixen</PackageTags>
        <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>

        <TargetsForTfmSpecificContentInPackage>
            $(TargetsForTfmSpecificContentInPackage);PackNetGenerators
        </TargetsForTfmSpecificContentInPackage>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!--
        ⚠ The replication and RPC generators travel with the package, which until now they did not,
        and the gap was silent in the way this one always is. Analyzers do not flow through a
        `ProjectReference`, so every in-tree consumer — `Samples/08`, the test projects — names
        `Vixen.Net.Generators` itself and everything is green; a project that restores the *package*
        got the runtime assembly and no generator, so its `[Replicated]` components and its
        `[ServerRpc]` methods were collected by nothing. No error: `ReplicatedComponents` and
        `RpcMethods` simply do not exist, which reads as the attributes being the thing that is
        missing. ⚠ `Vixen.Shaders`, `Vixen.Ui`, `Vixen.Input`, `Vixen.Engine`,
        `Vixen.Core.Reflection` and `Vixen.Core.Serialization` all pack theirs; this was the last
        exception, and `PackagedGeneratorTests` is what will now notice.

        This project deliberately does *not* reference the generator as an analyzer — see
        `NetworkTransformReplicator`, which is hand-written precisely because the engine does not run
        the generator over itself — so the target names it and asks only for its path.
        `GetTargetPath`, not `Build`: `NoBuild` is a global property, so asking for `Build` here
        fails a no-build `dotnet pack` with NETSDK1085.
    -->
    <Target Name="PackNetGenerators">
        <MSBuild Projects="..\Vixen.Net.Generators\Vixen.Net.Generators.csproj"
                 Targets="GetTargetPath"
                 Properties="Configuration=$(Configuration)"
                 RemoveProperties="TargetFramework">
            <Output TaskParameter="TargetOutputs" ItemName="NetGeneratorAssembly" />
        </MSBuild>

        <ItemGroup>
            <TfmSpecificPackageFile Include="@(NetGeneratorAssembly)" PackagePath="analyzers/dotnet/cs" />
        </ItemGroup>
    </Target>

    <ItemGroup>
        <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" Visible="false" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!--
            Replication is the reason: it queries the world's per-chunk change versions directly,
            which is the structural payoff doc 16 names for having built an ECS with them. Nothing
            below Vixen.Engine may reference this package in return — the layer rules in doc 00 run
            one way.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Core\Vixen.Core.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Core.Mathematics\Vixen.Core.Mathematics.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Ecs\Vixen.Ecs.csproj" />

        <!-- What the generated descriptors are registered into. -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Core.Reflection\Vixen.Core.Reflection.csproj" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!--
            ⚠ <b>Named here because <c>NetworkRules</c> is authored.</b> A <c>.vxnetrules</c> is a
            document somebody edits, and the editor's YAML binder resolves a member through a
            generated <c>TypeDescriptor</c> — which is emitted per assembly, because analyzers do not
            flow through a ProjectReference. Without this the nested `rules:` mapping in that document
            binds to nothing at all, with the same message the missing-analyzer case always gives:
            "nothing in this build claims the name".
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Core.Reflection.Generator\Vixen.Core.Reflection.Generator.csproj"
                          OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                          ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />

        <!--
            And the binary one, on Vixen.Water's terms: the policy is written into the content build
            as its serialized record because a dedicated server reads it back, and a type no
            serializer is registered for cannot be written by the importer or read by a game — on a
            trimmed build or otherwise.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Core.Serialization.Generator\Vixen.Core.Serialization.Generator.csproj"
                          OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                          ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Vixen.Net.Tests" />
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
